---
import LessonLayout  from '@/layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import LessonHeader  from '@/components/LessonHeader.astro';
import LessonDiagram from '@/components/LessonDiagram.astro';
import CodeBlock     from '@/components/CodeBlock.astro';
import LessonCallout from '@/components/LessonCallout.astro';
import LessonQuiz    from '@/components/LessonQuiz.astro';
import DeepDive      from '@/components/DeepDive.astro';
import LessonMindMap   from '@/components/LessonMindMap.astro';

const mindMap = `
# Memory and Conversation State
## Three Memory Types
### In-context: messages array
### Persistent: DB by thread ID
### Semantic: vector embeddings
## Context Window Limits
### Cost grows per turn
### Trim what you send
### Save full history separately
## Conversation Threading
### Unique thread ID per user
### Load then run then save
### Isolation by thread ID
## When to Use Each
### Single session: in-context
### Returning users: persistent
### Large history: semantic
## Tensions
### Memory freshness vs Retrieval latency
### High-k vs Low-k retrieval
### Stateful threads vs Stateless client
## Pitfalls
### Memory without forgetting
### Namespace collision multi-tenant
### Synchronous retrieval blocking responses
### Unbounded message history
### Thread ID collision across tenants
`;
---

<LessonLayout title="L4 — Memory & Conversation State"
>

  <LessonHeader
    num="L04"
    title="Memory & Conversation State"
    subtitle="Every agent so far forgets when the process exits. This lesson fixes that."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Three kinds of memory</h2>

    <div class="memory-grid">
      <div class="memory-card memory-card--blue">
        <div class="memory-card-label">Type 1</div>
        <div class="memory-card-title">In-context</div>
        <div class="memory-card-body">The <code>messages</code> array. Lives in RAM. Fast. Bounded by context window. Gone when the process exits.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="memory-card memory-card--green">
        <div class="memory-card-label">Type 2</div>
        <div class="memory-card-title">Persistent</div>
        <div class="memory-card-body">Messages saved to a DB (Postgres, Redis, file). Survives restarts. Loaded by thread ID. Backbone of multi-session chat.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="memory-card memory-card--purple">
        <div class="memory-card-label">Type 3</div>
        <div class="memory-card-title">Semantic</div>
        <div class="memory-card-body">Facts stored as vector embeddings. Retrieved by meaning, not exact key. Cross-session knowledge that scales indefinitely.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      You have been using Type 1 in every lesson so far. This lesson adds Type 2.
      Type 3 (vector memory) comes later once you have the foundation.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">The context window is not infinite</h2>

    <p>
      Every API call sends the full <code>messages</code> array. Cost and latency grow with every
      turn. For most agents the limits are irrelevant — but cost matters when you're sending 400
      old messages on every call.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Context window</th><th>Practical conversation limit</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>claude-haiku-4-5</td><td>200k tokens</td><td>~500 back-and-forth turns</td></tr>
          <tr><td>claude-sonnet-4-6</td><td>200k tokens</td><td>~500 turns, higher quality</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>Fix: trim the oldest messages once you exceed a threshold, or keep a rolling summary.</p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Conversation threading</h2>

    <p>
      A <strong>thread</strong> is one isolated conversation — like a single chat window.
      Each thread has a unique ID. On every request you load the thread, run the agent, then save.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  A["Request\n+ threadId"]:::step --> B["Load\nmessages"]:::step
  B --> C["Run\nagent"]:::step
  C --> D["Save\nmessages"]:::step
  D --> E["Return\nresponse"]:::done

  classDef step fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <p>
      In NestJS terms: the thread store is a repository. The agent service loads by
      <code>threadId</code>, runs the loop, persists the result. No new concepts —
      just a data-access pattern you already know.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Build it: stateful agent with file-based persistence</h2>

    <p>
      We use JSON files per thread — no external dependencies for now. In production, swap
      <code>loadThread</code> and <code>saveThread</code> for a Postgres repository.
      The agent code stays identical.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="javascript" label="stateful-agent.mjs" code={`
// Usage:
//   node --env-file=.env stateful-agent.mjs <threadId> "<message>"
//
// Try this sequence:
//   node --env-file=.env stateful-agent.mjs abc123 "My name is Derese"
//   node --env-file=.env stateful-agent.mjs abc123 "What is my name?"  ← new process, still remembers
//   node --env-file=.env stateful-agent.mjs xyz999 "What is my name?"  ← different thread, blank

import Anthropic from '@anthropic-ai/sdk';
import { readFileSync, writeFileSync, existsSync, mkdirSync } from 'fs';

const client = new Anthropic({ apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY });
mkdirSync('./threads', { recursive: true });

// ── THREAD STORE (swap this layer for Postgres/Redis in production) ───────────

function loadThread(threadId) {
  const path = \`./threads/\${threadId}.json\`;
  return existsSync(path) ? JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8')) : [];
}

function saveThread(threadId, messages) {
  writeFileSync(\`./threads/\${threadId}.json\`, JSON.stringify(messages, null, 2));
}

// ── CONTEXT TRIMMING (keep last N turns to control token cost) ────────────────

function trimToLastN(messages, n = 20) {
  return messages.length <= n ? messages : messages.slice(-n);
}

// ── AGENT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

async function chat(threadId, userMessage) {
  const history = loadThread(threadId);

  console.log(\`[thread:\${threadId}] \${history.length} prior messages\`);

  const messages = [
    ...trimToLastN(history),                   // past context, trimmed for cost
    { role: 'user', content: userMessage },    // new turn
  ];

  const response = await client.messages.create({
    model: 'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001',
    max_tokens: 1024,
    system: 'You are a helpful assistant with memory of this conversation.',
    messages,
  });

  const reply = response.content
    .filter(b => b.type === 'text')
    .map(b => b.text)
    .join('');

  console.log(\`User: \${userMessage}\`);
  console.log(\`Assistant: \${reply}\\n\`);

  // Save the FULL history (not trimmed) — trim only what we SEND to the API
  saveThread(threadId, [
    ...history,
    { role: 'user', content: userMessage },
    { role: 'assistant', content: reply },
  ]);

  return reply;
}

// ── ENTRYPOINT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

const [,, threadId, ...rest] = process.argv;
if (!threadId || !rest.length) {
  console.log('Usage: node stateful-agent.mjs <threadId> "<message>"');
  process.exit(1);
}
await chat(threadId, rest.join(' '));
    `} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Run it — across separate processes</h2>

    <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="terminal" code={`
node --env-file=.env stateful-agent.mjs abc123 "My name is Derese, I build NestJS apps"
node --env-file=.env stateful-agent.mjs abc123 "What do you know about me?"
node --env-file=.env stateful-agent.mjs xyz999 "What do you know about me?"
    `} />

    <CodeBlock lang="text" label="second call output" code={`
[thread:abc123] 2 prior messages
User: What do you know about me?
Assistant: You told me your name is Derese and that you build NestJS applications.
    `} />

    <CodeBlock lang="text" label="third call — different thread" code={`
[thread:xyz999] 0 prior messages
User: What do you know about me?
Assistant: I don't have any information about you yet. Feel free to share!
    `} />

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="The trim vs save distinction">
      <p>
        Trim what you <em>send to the API</em> to control cost.
        Save the <em>full history</em> to disk so you never lose turns.
        These are independent concerns.
      </p>
      <p>
        The code saves to <code>history</code> (full array) but sends
        <code>trimToLastN(history)</code> to the API.
        Old turns are preserved for auditing or replay. The trim window can be widened any time without data loss.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">When to add semantic memory (Type 3)</h2>

    <p>
      Persistent threads solve the session problem but not the <em>scale</em> problem. A user
      with 500 past conversations has ~10,000 messages — you cannot load all of them into context.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Single ongoing conversation</td><td>In-context (messages array)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Returning users across sessions</td><td>Persistent thread (DB)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Long history — retrieve only relevant past facts</td><td>Semantic / vector memory</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shared knowledge base across many users</td><td>Semantic / vector memory</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Quick-check quiz</h2>

    <LessonQuiz questions={[
      {
        q:       'You trim messages to the last 20 before the API call. Should you also trim what you save to the database?',
        options: [
          'No — save the full history; only trim what you send to the API',
          'Yes — save only the last 20 to keep the DB small',
          "It doesn't matter — the agent ignores old messages anyway",
          'Yes — older messages are never useful',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Trimming controls API cost. Saving preserves the full record. These are independent. Trimming the DB loses history permanently — you may need it for auditing, replay, or widening the trim window later.',
      },
      {
        q:       'Two users share the same agent but must not see each other\'s history. What mechanism isolates them?',
        options: [
          'Unique thread IDs — each user\'s messages are stored and loaded separately',
          'Running separate Node.js processes per user',
          'The LLM automatically ignores other users\' messages',
          'Clearing the messages array between requests',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Thread IDs are the isolation mechanism. Each user gets a unique thread ID; their messages are stored and loaded separately with no overlap.',
      },
      {
        q:       'A user has 500 past conversations totalling 50,000 messages. They ask "what did we discuss about billing last month?" What memory type is needed?',
        options: [
          'Semantic memory — embed and retrieve only the relevant past messages',
          'In-context — load all 50,000 messages into the array',
          'Persistent thread — load the latest thread only',
          'No memory needed — rephrase the question to include all context',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: '50,000 messages cannot fit in context. Semantic memory uses vector embeddings to retrieve only the handful of past messages relevant to the current question.',
      },
      {
        q:       'In a NestJS app, where does the thread store logic belong?',
        options: [
          'In a repository or service — the agent loop just receives and returns messages',
          'Inside the agent loop itself — it should handle its own persistence',
          'In a global singleton shared across all requests',
          'In the controller — closest to the HTTP layer',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Separation of concerns: a repository handles persistence; the agent service handles LLM orchestration. The agent loop should be decoupled from storage — swap DB implementations without touching the loop.',
      },
    ]} />

  </div>

  <DeepDive
    sources="l1_1.8, l2_2.11"
    tensions={[
      { a: 'Memory freshness',           b: 'Retrieval latency (+50–200ms)' },
      { a: 'High-k retrieval (recall)',  b: 'Low-k retrieval (clean context)' },
      { a: 'Synchronous memory writes',  b: 'Async writes (eventual consistency)' },
      { a: 'Stateful chatbot (threads)', b: 'Stateless (state on client)' },
    ]}
    questions={[
      {
        q:      'Design a memory system for a multi-tenant SaaS where User A\'s memories must never surface in User B\'s session.',
        hint:   'Isolation is a namespace problem. What keys do you use, and where do you enforce the filtering?',
        answer: '<p>Use hierarchical namespacing: <code>namespace = (tenant_id, workspace_id, "memories")</code>. Every write includes these namespace components; every read filters by the same namespace — physically impossible to retrieve cross-tenant memories.</p><p>Extract <code>tenant_id</code> and <code>workspace_id</code> from the authenticated request context, never from user-provided input. Validate at both write and read paths.</p><p>Audit: log every memory write and read with the namespace. A cross-namespace access in logs is a critical security incident.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'How do you handle conflicting memories? User stored "prefers Python" in January and "now works in Go" in March.',
        hint:   'Conflicts are temporal — the newer one is usually correct. But "usually" is not "always."',
        answer: '<p><strong>Recency-weighted retrieval:</strong> store a timestamp with every memory. Surface the newer one with higher priority. Include timestamps so the LLM can reason: "User prefers Python (Jan 2024), User now works in Go (Mar 2024)."</p><p><strong>Update-on-conflict:</strong> when writing a new memory, detect if it contradicts an existing one. If yes, update the existing memory and store the change history.</p><p><strong>Explicit contradiction flag:</strong> mark conflicting memories and surface both with a note: "There is a conflict — the more recent preference is Go." Let the LLM handle resolution contextually.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'How do you implement conversation summarisation when message history grows too long?',
        hint:   'The history needs to shrink without losing important context.',
        answer: '<p>Add a check-and-trim step before the main LLM call. If <code>token_count(messages) > 75% of context window</code>: run a cheap summarisation call — "Summarise the key facts and decisions from this conversation in 200 words."</p><p>Replace old messages with: <code>[{ role: "user", content: "Previous conversation summary: {summary}" }, ...last 5 messages]</code>.</p><p>Keep the last 5 messages verbatim — most relevant for continuity. The summary captures key facts without verbatim dialogue. Track: how often summarisation triggers and response quality on queries referencing summarised content.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'When should you use vector store (semantic) vs key-value vs SQL vs knowledge graph memory?',
        hint:   'Each storage type has a query pattern it excels at. Match query pattern to storage type.',
        answer: '<p><strong>Vector store:</strong> "find memories semantically related to this context" — fuzzy, meaning-based retrieval. Use for user preferences, past experiences.</p><p><strong>Key-value:</strong> "fetch this specific fact by ID instantly" — low latency, exact match. Use for user timezone, subscription tier, last session timestamp.</p><p><strong>SQL:</strong> "find all memories where topic=X and date>Y" — structured, filterable, aggregatable. Use for interaction history, event logs, auditable records.</p><p><strong>Knowledge graph:</strong> "traverse relationships between entities" — user works at company X, company X uses technology Y. Use when connections matter as much as facts.</p><p>Most production systems use 2–3 of these in combination.</p>',
      },
    ]}
    pitfalls={[
      'Memory without forgetting — a store that only grows eventually surfaces outdated, contradictory memories. Implement memory decay, importance scoring, or explicit TTLs from day one.',
      'Namespace collision in multi-tenant systems — flat keys (<code>memory_123</code>) instead of hierarchical namespacing (<code>user_A/workspace_1/memory_123</code>) means memories can bleed across tenants. Retrofit is painful.',
      'Synchronous memory retrieval blocking responses — calling the memory store before every response adds 150–300ms to P50 latency. Parallelize: start memory retrieval in parallel with other init steps.',
      'Unbounded message history — <code>messages</code> accumulates forever. After a long conversation it fills the context window. Implement sliding window trimming or summarisation for long-running sessions.',
      'Thread ID collision in multi-tenant systems — simple IDs (<code>session_1</code>) collide across tenants. Always namespace: <code>customer_A_session_1</code>.',
    ]}
  />

  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

</LessonLayout>

